meta {
  name: 13 CreateEquivalentEntries
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/equivalent-entries
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  [
    {
      "existingIdInSource": "234443",
      "existingSourceId": "System A",
      "registryId": "{{registryId}}",
      "categoryId": "{{categoryId}}",
      "categorySourceId": "{{categorySourceId}}",
      "entry": {
        "idInSource": "TIC-106",
        "sourceId": "System D",
        "name": "Top Temp Control"
      }
    }
  ]
}

script:post-response {
  // The new entry should have adopted ciridA from the existing entry.
  bru.setVar("expectedCirid", bru.getEnvVar("ciridA"));
}

tests {
  test("returns 201", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });
}

docs {
  §3.1.2, the semantic core of the specification: this is where equivalence is
  actually asserted. Everything else manages a registry.

  Three CIRID merge rules:

  1. The existing Entry has a CIRID -> the new Entry adopts it, and any CIRID
     supplied on the new Entry is discarded.
  2. Only the new Entry has one -> it propagates BACKWARD onto the existing
     Entry. This modifies a record the caller never asked to change, and is the
     rule implementations most often get wrong.
  3. Neither has one -> the server mints a UUID and assigns it to both.

  Here entry 234443/System A already carries ciridA, so rule 1 applies and
  TIC-106/System D joins that equivalence set. Verify with request 13a.
}
